Support infill: work out the arch cost limits on every call.

connect_base_support() kept three limits in function-level statics, so the
first support fill of the process fixed them for every later one, although
they scale with that fill's line spacing (base and interface spacing differ).
Which fill ran first depends on how the layers were split between threads,
and the browser engine keeps them for the whole tab: the same supported
Benchy sliced 6049.6 mm fresh and 6026.3 mm after a slice with another
support spacing. Same change as OrcaSlicer #15564 (after 2.4.2).

--- a/src/libslic3r/Fill/FillBase.cpp
+++ b/src/libslic3r/Fill/FillBase.cpp
@@ -2453,9 +2453,11 @@
 #endif // INFILL_DEBUG_OUTPUT
 
     const std::vector<SupportArcCost> arches = evaluate_support_arches(infill_ordered, graph, spacing, params);
-    static const double cost_low      = line_spacing * 1.3;
-    static const double cost_high     = line_spacing * 2.;
-    static const double cost_veryhigh = line_spacing * 3.;
+    // Must not be static: line_spacing differs per call (base and interface
+    // fills), a static keeps whichever call ran first (OrcaSlicer #15564).
+    const double cost_low      = line_spacing * 1.3;
+    const double cost_high     = line_spacing * 2.;
+    const double cost_veryhigh = line_spacing * 3.;
 
     {
         std::vector<const SupportArcCost*> selected;
